      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Battery Groups Cluster · Last reviewed 2026-09-23</p>
      <h1>Battery Group Size vs Performance: What a Group Number Does — and Does Not — Guarantee</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A BCI group number is a <strong>fit specification</strong>: case dimensions, terminal type and polarity.
        It is not a power specification. The same group number can be built to 600 CCA or 900 CCA, 50 Ah or
        100 Ah — and the difference is not marketing noise. It is construction, and it is visible in the
        datasheet if you know where to look.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Group size fixes the box; the build fixes the power. Larger groups
        generally offer more capacity and CCA because the case holds more plate material, but within any one
        group, <strong>plate count, plate thickness, flooded-vs-AGM and quality choices</strong> shift the
        ratings materially. Buy the group for the fit, and the <em>ratings for the duty</em>.
      </div>

      <h2>What the group number actually standardizes</h2>
      <p>
        The Battery Council International group system exists so that any battery labelled
        <a href="/lead-acid/battery-group-sizes/">Group 24</a>, say, fits any Group 24 tray: length, width,
        height, terminal arrangement and polarity are defined. What it does <em>not</em> define is how much
        lead is inside, how the plates are built, or what the battery must deliver. Two Group 24s can sit in
        the same tray and perform very differently — which is precisely what
        <a href="/lead-acid/group-24-vs-27/">Group 24 vs 27</a> comparisons are really about: the larger case
        <em>enables</em> more performance, it does not mandate it.
      </p>

      <h2>How the same group produces different ratings</h2>
      <p>
        Three construction choices move the numbers inside a fixed case:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Plate count vs thickness.</strong> A starting build packs many thin plates to maximize
          surface area — high <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">CCA</a>, modest Ah. A
          <a href="/lead-acid/deep-cycle-vs-starting/">deep-cycle</a> build uses fewer, thicker plates — more
          Ah and endurance, lower CCA. Same group, opposite priorities.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Flooded vs AGM.</strong> An <a href="/lead-acid/agm-battery/">AGM</a> build of a group
          typically supports higher charge acceptance and better vibration resistance than a flooded build, and
          the two can carry different ratings despite identical case dimensions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Material quality.</strong> Grid alloy, paste density, separator grade and welding quality
          all move real performance — and real
          <a href="/testing/internal-resistance-matters/">internal resistance</a> — even when the label
          numbers look the same.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Group → typical performance map</h2>
      <p>
        The table shows typical ranges for the most common groups. These are <strong>typical build ranges</strong>,
        not guarantees — within every group, starting, deep-cycle and AGM variants sit at different points.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Group</th>
            <th>Typical case</th>
            <th>Typical Ah range</th>
            <th>Typical CCA range</th>
            <th>Typical duty</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>H5 / L2 (Group 47)</strong></td>
            <td>242 × 175 mm</td>
            <td>~55–60 Ah</td>
            <td>~500–650</td>
            <td>Compact car starting</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>H6 / L3 (Group 48)</strong></td>
            <td>278 × 175 mm</td>
            <td>~60–70 Ah</td>
            <td>~600–760</td>
            <td>Mid-size car starting</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>H7 / L4 (Group 94R)</strong></td>
            <td>315 × 175 mm</td>
            <td>~70–80 Ah</td>
            <td>~760–850</td>
            <td>Large car / SUV starting</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>H8 / L5 (Group 49)</strong></td>
            <td>354 × 175 mm</td>
            <td>~90–95 Ah</td>
            <td>~850–900</td>
