      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Typical ranges compiled from the site's group pages and manufacturer fitment literature — use them to
        orient, then verify against the specific datasheet, because the same group spans multiple builds.
      </p>

      <h2>The H-family: same width, growing length</h2>
      <p>
        The H5→H8 progression shows the mapping logic cleanly: all four share the same 175 mm width and terminal
        layout, and each step adds case length — about 36 mm from H5 to H6, then to H7, then to H8. Each step
        buys room for more plate area, and the typical Ah and CCA step up with it. The fit difference is
        described in <a href="/lead-acid/h7-h8-battery/">H7 vs H8</a>; the point here is that within the
        H-family, <strong>bigger group ≈ bigger ratings</strong> holds — but only <em>on average</em>. A premium
        H6 build can out-rate a budget H7, because construction outranks case size once the build quality gap
        widens.
      </p>

      <h2>What the group number cannot tell you</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Duty design.</strong> Whether a Group 24 is a starting, deep-cycle or dual-purpose build is
          not in the group number — it is in the plate design.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Actual tested ratings.</strong> Two labels can print the same CCA under different standards
          (<a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">JIS vs EN vs SAE</a>), so the printed figure still needs its
          standard.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Quality and longevity.</strong> Warranty length, <a href="/testing/cycle-life-measured/">cycle life</a>
          and calendar life are set by construction quality, invisible in the group name.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Buy the group for <strong>fit</strong> — verify tray dimensions, hold-down and terminal layout against the drawing, not just the group name.</li>
        <li>Then buy the <strong>ratings for the duty</strong>: cranking CCA for starting, Ah and RC for deep-cycle/standby, from the specific datasheet, with the standard named.</li>
        <li>When two suppliers quote the same group at different prices, compare <strong>build spec</strong> — plate type, AGM vs flooded, warranty — not just the printed CCA.</li>
        <li>Ask whether the quoted rating is the <strong>production-unit tested value</strong> or a catalogue family figure.</li>
        <li>For mixed fleets, consider <strong>group consolidation</strong>: standardizing on one or two groups simplifies stock, but only where fit genuinely allows it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Does a bigger group size mean more power?</h3>
      <p>Usually, but not automatically. Group size fixes the physical case, so a larger group has room for more plate material and therefore typically offers higher capacity and CCA. But construction — plate thickness, paste density, flooded vs AGM — moves the ratings substantially within the same group, so two batteries of the same group size can differ measurably in performance.</p>
      <h3>Why do batteries of the same group size have different CCA and Ah?</h3>
      <p>Because group size standardizes fit, not construction. A starting battery in a group uses many thin plates for cranking surface (high CCA, modest Ah); a deep-cycle battery in the same case uses fewer, thicker plates (more Ah and endurance, lower CCA). Quality, paste, separators and grid alloy move the numbers further.</p>
      <h3>Can I fit a higher-performance battery in the same group?</h3>
      <p>Within limits, yes. As long as the group size, terminal type and polarity match the vehicle, a higher-CCA or higher-Ah build of the same group will fit. The real constraint is the tray, hold-down and cable length — always verify physical fit against the manufacturer drawing, not just the group name.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
